= main =
Why I believe that no extensional account can work.
Not only may S believe that A will occur
but S may also believe that if A does not occur then B will. This cannot be
captured as believing that ¬A ⊃ B, since that only requires believing A
(strongly). Updating our beliefs by conditionalisation requires 

= notes on first reading =
== chapter 2 ==
*problems with hypothetico-deductivism and fellow travellers*
Technically correct but uninspiring criticisms of earlier attempts to demarcate
the conditions under which evidence E supports/confirms hypothesis H.  While it
does accurately point out the flaws in various hypothetico-deductive accounts it
does not convince me that no such approach can work. Though it does convince me
that some kind of modal logic is required (though modal logic shows us how this
can be turned into first order logic).

=== start ===
    how can evidence stated in one language confirm hypotheses stated in a
    language that outstrips the first? 
    (fp. 10)
This is Glymour's way of putting the question that i pose as what is evidence
for believing there are objects on the far side of an epistemic bottleneck.

=== four strategies ===
	1) Eliminate theory (reductionism - all meaningful statements can be reduced
	   to statements about sense data/phenomena/???)
	2) Hypothetico-deductivism (Theory implies observation so observations
	   support theory)
	3) Bootstrap: Carnapian - auxiliary analytic hypotheses containing both
	   theoretical and observational terms connect claims containing
	   purely theoretical claims to purely observable claims (evidence).
	4) Probabilistic strategies (Bayesian? Any others?)

=== rejecting reichenbachs account ===
    a claim in the larger language is true if and only if all of its
    (first-order) observational consequences are true.
    (fp. 14)
Glymour points out this immediately leads to trouble:
	Let O be an observational predicate and T be a theoretical one
suppose we observe that ∃x¬O(x). ∀xT(x) has no observational consequences and so
(according to the claim) is true. So too, ∀xT(x) ⊃ ∀yO(y) has no observational
consequences so it is true as well. But the conjunction of the two implies
something we observe to be false.

Then he immediately dismisses the defence that this standard should only be
applied to whole theories on the following two grounds:
    a) we reason about the consequences of hypotheses that have
       not been incorporated into any theory,
We reason about them alright, but maybe we don't evaluate how well supported
they are by evidence? This is perhaps due to him casting this problem as
suggesting that _modus ponens_ does not preserve truth.
    b) we are often concerned with investigating the consequences of conjoining
       two otherwise independent theories—relativity and quantum mechanics, for
       example. An account of truth ought to permit that such reasoning can be
       truth preserving, 
Um... we can indeed explore their logical consequences. This dismissal of holism
doesnt connect with me.
I suppose that we combine two theories that are both well supported by evidence
(all their observational consequences are true) but together they imply some
false observational sentences. But all the "purely observational" claims are
true, hence the contradition cannot arise there. So it must instead hinge on
some theoretical term. But this can be diagnosed as an ambiguity - the quantum
and the relativistic notions of "mass" are different. Differentiate them so the
same term does not occur in both and the contradiction is removed.

Glymour's problem is that we can't tell what sentences "follow from" others
because even _modus ponens_ doesnt preserve truth. But we are stipulating that
theories are closed under logical deduction. Its seems this is founded in
differene attitudes towards logic - Glymour taking it as a study of truth
preserving inferences, where holists take logic as the building material (lego
blocks) from which theories are constructed.

:RLN: there is something significant near this here - we must take peoples'
beliefs as probability distributions over theories
-> [[confirmation_holism]], [[propositional_attitudes]]

=== reductive elimination/definition ===
    In the first place, there is no reason to believe that, for every case in
    which a piece of evidence bears upon a piece of theory, a correspondence of
    the kind required can be located.
    (fp. 17)
Indeed - gussied up a bit this is the basis of my confirmation holism. How does
Glymour avoid it?

(fp. 19) Anti-Carnap: this is (3) above
Both theoretical claims and the observation claims to which they are reduced are
synthetic. But the sentneces that effect this reduction by connecting
theoretical and observational terms are _analytic_. True by stipulation.
(a la Wittgensteinian criteria). The Quinean position is that we cannot
distinguish criteria from common knowledge.

Interesting that Glymour here emphasises another use for analyticity. Not only
was it supposed to explain our knowledge of necessary truths, but also to serve
as the connectors between observations and theoretical truths.

=== Ramsey sentences as eliminations ===
see [[lewis1972-psychophysicalAndTheoreticalIdentifications]]
I think these work but (putting aside 2nd order quantification [e.g. over
predicates]) we still need to quantify over the theoretical objects. They are
not eliminated but referred to by means of a huge definite description.

Glymours issue is similar to [[#rejecting reichenbachs account]]. Again he is
concerned with truth preserving inference.
Does A, B |- A∧B ? (Does the inference preserve truth.)
Glymour claims not. Basically because when we replace a theoretical predicate in
A and B with a quantified 2nd order variable it might not be the same in both.

But this doesn't even go through if we take (as per Lewis) the requirement of
unique realisation.

In any case, we can do the following:
$[x] is a finite tuple of variables (x1...xn), potentially second order$
for A[x1,x2 ...] when expanded we have ∃[v]{ ∃[x](T[x] and [x] = [v]) and A[v] }
the first clause fixes the references of the theoretical terms, but doesn't
include A[v] which is couched in terms from the theory _T_ but does not
contribute to the reference fixing. Still relys on uniqueness.

[!Interesting that Glymour leans heavily on validity and rationality. Precisely
the things I wish to abandon.!]

=== deOccamized theories ===
As Glymour expects I have no problem with these theories.

The challenge is to explain why simplicity is a guide to truth (and steers
away from false theories) when they are not empirically equivalent. Objects
leave traces and it is implausible that an object has no other manifestation
that this particular one.

=== Popper counter example ===
(fp. 34)
The counter example is dense. To make it comprehensible lets make the following
substitutions:
$C$ for $A$
$C ⊃ T$ for $S$
$¬C ∧ O'1 ∧ O'2 ∧ ... ∧ O'n$ for $B$

The five conditions thus become
1. |- $T$ === $C ⊃ T .∧ C$
$C$ is theorem of $T$, hence so is $T ⊃ C$
$T$ is a theorem of $T$ and hence so is $C ⊃ T$

2. $C ⊃ T .∧ C$  |- $O1 O2 ... On$ 
fine we can assume this

3. $C ⊃ T .∧ ¬C ∧ O'1 ∧ O'2 ∧ ... ∧ O'n$ |- $O'1 ∧ O'2 ∧ ... ∧ O'n$

4. $¬C ∧ O'1 ∧ O'2 ∧ ... ∧ O'n$ |- $¬C$

5. $O'i$ |- $¬Oi$

And from all this we should
reject $C$ (and $C ⊃ T$)
in favour of $¬C ∧ O'1 ∧ O'2 ∧ ... ∧ O'n$ (and $C ⊃ T$ )

So our alternative theory is $O'1 ... O'n ∧ ¬C$
and $S$ which is supposed to be the _same_ is $C ⊃ T$ which before revision we
believe because we believe $T$ is true and afterwards because we believe $C$ is
false.
But it seems that we want an S such that S ∧ A ⊃ T regardless of whether T is
true or not.

Note also that the new theory is simply ¬C and the conjunct of all O'1...O'n.
So the real problem which emerges when the O'i s are not recursively decidable
isn't touched upon.

Still, there may be something deeper here. Maybe we cannot define good
inference/updating in the face of new evidence purely in terms of how things
are in this single actual world. We define a space of possibilities that we are
confident include this world (a synthetic hypothesis if you will) and then
reason within that. Such definition need not be taken as reflecting a
modal truth, nor require non-extensional terms.

=== skipping Kepler and Hemple because it is tedious ===

=== Carnaps proposal ===
also uninspiring - lets get to the positive proposal

== chapter 3 ==
*problems with Bayesianism*
=== probabilistic arguments rare in history of science ===
<q>Copernicus, Newton, Kepler, none of them give probabilistic arguments for
their theories, nor does Maxwell or Kelvin or Lavoisier or Dalton or Einstein or
Schrodinger or ...</q>

On the other hand there are jokes about experimental physicists being in the job
of manufacturing error bars. Even Kepler may be viewed as making a probabilistic
argument about the eight arc-minute discrepancy in observations of Mar's
position (Kepler: <q>If I had believed we could ignore these eight minutes, I
would have patched up my hypothesis. But since it was not permissible to ignore,
those eight minutes pointed the road to a complete reformation of
astronomy.</q>)

There is an implicit understanding that observations are subject to extraneous
noise, and how far off an observation can be from predicted values before it
confutes them.

=== lack of explanation ===
Bayesian accounts don't explain why we:
	* value variety of evidence
	* eschew ad hoc hypotheses
	* how to distinguish tacked on hypotheses from integral ones
	* why particular evidence is related to parituclar hypothese

But they do explain why we eschew ad hoc hypotheses (at least as I understand
them [[confirmation#ad hoc hypotheses]]) if the prior probability of E|H is less
than the probability of E then in the face of E I should reduce my confidence in
H. But with an ad hoc hypothesis I come up with a reason not to reduce my
confidence in H.

I currently think the rest are more or less resolved (apart from our credences
not being quite probabilities)

=== credences are not probabiliities ===
[[degrees_of_belief]]

=== Bayesianism leaves no room for reasoning ===
On this one I think I agree.

=== why theorise ===
    A theory is never any better established than is the collection of its
    observational consequences.  Why, then, should we entertain theories at all?
    On the probabilist view, it seems, they are a gratuitous risk. The natural
    answer is that theories have some special function that their collection of
    observational consequences cannot serve; the function most frequently
    suggested is explanation—theories explain, their collection of observational
    consequences do not. But however sage this suggestion may be, it only makes
    more vivid the difficulty of the Bayesian way of seeing things. ... The
    Bayesian account of confirmation makes it impossible for a piece of evidence
    to give us more total credence in a theory than in its observational
    consequences. <br>
    (fp. 83-84)

What a good question! Lucky I have a good answer, see [[confirmation_holism]]

=== old evidence can confirm new theories ===
:RLN: perhaps good for #whither_philosophy - and as a later example of reasoning
    Copernicus argued for his theory using observations made over the course of
    millenia, not on the basis of any startling new predictions derived from the
    theory ... .  Newton argued for universal gravitation using Kepler’s second
    and third laws, established before the Principia was published. The argument
    that Einstein gave in 1915 for his gravitational field equations was that
    they explained the anomalous advance of the perihelion of Mercury,
    established more than half a century earlier. ... Old evidence can in fact
    confirm new theory, but according to Bayesian kinematics it cannot. 
    (fp. 86)

Think I am happy with my solution here too.
Inchoate theory. [[non-linguistic_representation]]

He does a nice job of arguing that the idea of a counterfactual how much
confidecne we would have had in a theory (if we had thought of it?) on the
observing the old evidence doesn't really make sense.

== chapter 4 ==
*problems for Kuhn's followers*
(since Kuhn does not address <q>the question of what makes evidence relevant to
theory [or] what makes for better evidence</q> he has no account of confirmation
to be questioned or refuted. Still Kuhn can (I think) be regarded as attempting
to develop a account of confirmation based upon the observed history of science.
(What did scientists do that convinced others to accept their claims/theories?)

But followers of Kuhn who try to develop his account into something that
addresses the issue of what confirms what and how scientists use evidence to
justify their claims and convince their peers CAN be crticised, and so Glymour
does. But these criticisms are mainly ones mentioned above.

== chapter 5 ==
*positive account begins*
computations and representations
=== equation version ===
A *theory* is a set of equations.

A *computation* is a tree in which the nodes are quantities (variables contained
in the theories equations, e.g. constants whose value is determined by
experiment, or quantitative properties of the objects that the theory concerns)
whose values can be determined from the values satisfying their child nodes and
the equations of the theory.

*zero-level nodes* are experimentally determined quantities. (E.g. the weight,
color, length or position of an ordinary middle sized object.)

It seems that any *computation tree* can be flattened into just two levels (zero
and first) with all the zero-level nodes on which the computation is based
retained, and one first-level node for each quantity (variable) to which the
theory assigns a value. (Which can be deduced from the zero-level values by use
of the theory equations.)

So we cannot in general speak of a given quantity as being of a certain level
(always being represented by a node with the same level in every computation).
Nor even say that a particular quantity (variable) is of a higher level than
another (unless every computation that fixes the former also fixes the latter).
~~and, as might be expected, Glymour goes on to do exactly this on page 124~~

So we must regard having a *multi-level computation* (as in the given examples)
as a common practice rather than a requirement. Scientists often rely upon
theory and evidence to deduce the value of particular quantities that objects in
that theory are related to. The gravitational constant, the mass of any ordinary
object (even one whose mass we cant directly or easily determine). They then use
that quantity in combination with further evidence to deduce predictions of
future observations. Determining (the numerical value of) a single property
often only involves a fragment of a particular theory.

If we did take having a multi-level tree as a requirement then it would mean
that each quantity would have to be deducible (perhaps indirectly) from theory
and the quantities of zero-level nodes. (However, this is also true of the
flattened tree, the representation.) And in each step of the method by which it
is derived a value of a quantity is newly determined.
This would seem to preclude a simultaenous equation with several solutions.
That might be the result of some experiment, and then some later experiment
might either add another constraint, or somehow eliminate some (all but one) of
those solutions.

==== a weird thing happened on the way to the office ====
An odd transition  happens on page 114. Up until this point Glymour has been
discussing the computation of qualities. Which is the deduction of the values
of experimental constants (e.g. k in PV=kT, the temperature generalisation of
Boyle's law, or the gravitational constant, or the mass [or other quantitative
property] of a particular object). On the one hand, each computation (every
example Glymour gives, but not in principle every instance) involves only a
fraction of a theory. We use only a handful (some?) of theoretical principles
and empirical measurements to derive the values of theoretical quantities.

In the case of PV=kT - we were told that this was the _entirety_ of the theory
    To take a very simple example, suppose the theory consists of the single
    hypothesis that for any sample of gas, so long as no gas is added to or
    removed from the sample, the product of the pressure and volume of the gas
    is proportional to the temperature of the gas. In other terms, under the
    given conditions (fp.111)
And in Glymour's other examples too there is no special part of the theory, the
_hypothesis_ that is specially being tested, as opposed to the rest of the
theory. But then all of a sudden it is a matter of individual hypotheses being
confirmed:
    More particularly, if experiment permits the computation of values for all
    quantities occurring in an hypothesis, and these values accord with the
    hypothesis, does the positive instance thus obtained support or confirm the
    hypothesis?
    (fp.114)

And then a equation is confirmed *with respect to a* theory:
    For a set of values of quantities to confirm an equation with respect to a
    theory consisting of a set of equations ... the set of values should be
    consistent with the theory; from the values for the initial quantities, a
    value for each quantity in the equation tested should be computable; the
    computation should involve only equations in the theory or their
    consequences; the equation that represents the hypothesis tested for the set
    of computations used should not be a mathematical identity; and the values
    obtained from the computations should constitute a solution to the equation
    that is tested.
    (fp.117)

Until finally on page 120:
    One also sees that the same hypothesis may be tested in different ways—that
    is, by means of different auxiliary hypothese
We started off by testing whole theories. At most we were testing hypotheses
*together with* theories. We have ended up testing hypotheses *by means of*
auxiliary hypotheses.  But no justification has been given for supposing that we
are specially testing the hypothesis (or have some special reason for rejecting
it when we compute values that are not a solution to it) rather than some other
part of the theory or the theory as a `whole.

=== there must be evidence which confutes ===
[[if_some_experiences_confirm_others_must_infirm]]
Going back a step, Glymour adds an additional requirement for a set of
observations to confirm a theory. To confirm a theory(/hypothesis) a set of observations must allow the
computation of various theoretical quantities (quantities that cannot simply be
<q>read-off</q> [in some sense] from observations) which fulfill/satisfy that
theory(/hypothesis). *However*, in addition the Cartesian product of possible
measurements must include some combinations that would falsify the
theory/hypothesis.
 
[[confirmation]] [[confirmation_holism]]
:RLN:important7: first set up the space of possibilities - only against that
space can we speak of a hypothesis/theory being confirmed at all

*a related problem*
==== what is the same method ====
Each of the values in J must be one that can be obtained by the same kind of
instrument or procedure as is used in obtaining the corresponding value in /,
from the same kind of system 

this is where tacking might come into account.


=== the bootstrapping condition ===
*This is the technical heart of Glymour's positive account*
The tie to Hempel's satisfaction condition si supposed to be replaceable.

Let E and H be sentences, T a consistent, deductively closed collection of
sentences. E confirms H with respect to T if:
i) T is consistent with H & E.
ii) The second condition says, in effect, that every quantity occurring in the
	hypothesis can be computed from the evidence, using the theory. 
iii) Hempel's satisfaction condition
iv) is little more than the requirement that the computations not guarantee that
	the hypothesis will te confirmed (or at least not disconfirmed), whatever
	the evidence might say.
v) is needed in order to ensure that the computations do not guarantee part of
   what the hypothesis asserts, only testing a remainder.

=== supposed virtues ===
==== scientific anti-holism ====
(relevance)
:good-point: Glymour's account does seem to give a good sense of why scientists
may feel holism is at odds with their normal practice. I don't <q>test the whole
theory</q> when I determine the mass of my car, nor even of Mars. I use some of
the theory to determine the value of some qualities and when they give me
inconsistent values or values that do not satisfy the theoretical constraints
then they cast doubt on _the part of the theory_ used to compute(/deduce) those
values from the observed quantities. If the values computed satisfy the
hypothesis then the evidence upon which that computation was based confirm that
hypothesis (plus, one imagines [though Perhaps Glymour does not] the part of the
theory used to compute those values); and if they do not satisfy the hypothesis
then the evidence upon which the computation was based infirm it (again plus
the fragment of supporting theory, though the drop in credence need not be
the same).
